            "The term **Realtor** refers to membership in a trade association rather than to the occupation itself. The association created the term in 1916, and for twenty-six years its written ethics treated the sale of a house to a Black family in a white neighborhood as a violation. On **June 6, 1924**, the **National Association of Real Estate Boards adopted Article 34** of its Code of Ethics: **\"A Realtor should never be instrumental in introducing into a neighborhood a character of property or occupancy, members of any race or nationality, or any individuals whose presence will clearly be detrimental to property values in that neighborhood.\"** The booklet listed the association's address as **310 South Michigan Avenue, Chicago**, about seven miles north of this block. Its bylaws required each member board to adopt the code, allowed discipline for violations, and permitted the national association to expel boards that refused to enforce it.",
            "Chicago's real estate industry had already adopted similar policies. In **1917, the Chicago Real Estate Board** approved a plan to fill the city **block by block**, and on **May 4, 1921**, it voted to expel members who sold to Black buyers on all-white blocks. Real estate education repeated the same claim. **Frederick Babcock's 1924 Appraisal of Real Estate** stated that \"residential values are affected by racial and religious factors.\" Babcock joined the **Federal Housing Administration in 1934**, led its underwriting division, and saw the federal government's 1938 manual restate his ideas. Explicit racial language was removed from the national code in **1950**, two years after the Supreme Court made restrictive covenants unenforceable. The national realtors' association apologized for this history on **November 19, 2020**.",
